A||||15||fiero56||involved brake m/c sizing question||||1||
Z||000000||fiero56||04-12-2001||12:11 PM||||Looking for replacement master cylinder for my stock system, and had WTIII run some numbers. Here is the info, he needs to double check pad clearance, and have everything double checked to make sure everything is right.<P>Drew,<P>I am having a bit of a problem with the calculations for the new master <BR>cylinder.<P>Your 88 MC has a bore of 24 mm (.94488") and stroke of 40 mm (1.57480")<BR>This makes the area of the MC = .70122603754512 in^2 & the Volume of the MC <BR>= 1.1042907639261 in^3 using the simple formula Area = pi*r^2 and Volume = <BR>pi*r^2*stroke<P>Also you should note that you have a 3.5:1 pedal arc ratio.  This means that <BR>100 # input force from your foot is a 350# input to the MC.<P>The force output from the MC is 499.1257901#<P>The problem came into play when I tried to calculate volume reqirements of <BR>the system.  Each wheel cylinder has a bore of 48mm (1.88976in).  The stroke <BR>is not included in the MVMA manual.  The area of the Wheel Cyl is <BR>2.80481487035904 in^2.  To try to calculate the stroke of the cylinder I <BR>calculated the maximum thickness of the rotor and brake pads.  The rotor on <BR>your car is 19mm (.75") and both pads are listed as 5mm (.19685 in) and <BR>5.85mm (.2303149in).  This means that when the three are closed you have a <BR>total thickness of 29.85 mm which I approximated at 30mm (1.1810).  I had to <BR>guesstimate the clearance between the adjustable pads and the rotor.  I <BR>estimated a worst case scenario of .030".  I guessed this since it is the <BR>max. radial runout on the rotors before they are considered out of spec.  I <BR>guessed that their spec is goverened by their max clearance.  Using that <BR>assumption, each wheel cyl requires approximately .0841444461107712 in^3 <BR>volume to clamp down and all four require .336577784430848 in^3 from the MC.<P>If that is correct, you could go with virtually any MC out there.  For <BR>example if we choose the 7/8" bore x 1.2" stroke MC we get an area of <BR>.601341015625 in^2 and a volume of .72160921875 in^3.  Assuming the same <BR>input force of 350#, you get an output force of 582.03247#.<P>If you don't mind, can you run research the forum and  <A HREF="http://www.grassrootsmotorsports.com?" TARGET=_blank>www.grassrootsmotorsports.com?</A>   If you just post them in the forums asking <BR>folks to validate my assumption of .030" I think that we will be in <BR>business.  Also ask why the GM MC would have a volume of 1.10429... in^3 <BR>when only .3365777... in^3 is required.<P>THX,<P>William||||reg||1
Z||000001||Carrolles||04-12-2001||03:28 PM||||Don't forget to consider pedal travel when doing your calcs.  You don't want the pedal to hit the floor before the brakes are clamped.  This sometimes happens when using bigger bore wheel cylinders/caliper pistons  than the MC was designed for.||||reg||1
Z||000002||william_thorniii||04-12-2001||04:09 PM||||Carrolles,<P>That should not happen in this case since Drew intends on using the same pedal ratio and wheel cyl bore as stock.  Thus all that changes is the volume.  A mere change in volume (of the MC) should not change the amount of fluid that is required within the system to clamp the brakes.  Good point though.. can you imagine the maiden voyage of a sweet ride with all new brakes.... that could not stop?<P>William||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||04-12-2001||04:54 PM||||I placed the order this afternoon. I called about the Wilwood cylinder first. Price was $53, but they were out of stock at the moment and the new employee I spoke with didn't know how long it would take to special order. I don't want to wait, so I called about the Tilton next. Price was $67 and they had 2 in stock. Gave c/c info, and it is on its way. I didn't ask how long to ship it out, but hopefully gets here soon. <P>$67 sure beats the cheapest ac/delco unit I found for $153. <P>The pedal force should be close, but just slightly softer with slightly more pedal travel. Cylinder bore is 22.225mm, instead of stock 24mm.<P>The front end should be good for a while.<BR>new rotors on front(special order)<BR>new organic pads on front(took almost a week to track them down, special order)<BR>new master cylinder, took a few days to determine sizing(special order)<BR>cooling system got a new radiator(special order)<BR>isn't owning an '88 pure joy <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><BR><p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 04-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phil||04-12-2001||06:50 PM||||You might want to rethink the stroke of the caliper piston. The stroke is the total distance of the pads to the rotor which in a perfect system would be 0" , but with wobble and the like and allowing a bit for pad and componnent compression the stroke should only be a millimeter or so.||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||04-12-2001||07:51 PM||||Go read the brake upgrade article again. You messed up your volume calculations baddly. You forgot to take into acount:<P>1. the circuit needing the most volume should be connected to the MC primary outlet. The exact oposite of a stock fiero...<P>1A. The stroke is not always the same for each circuit. Also that number is usually maximum stroke. You don't want to try to use all of it.<P>2. the available volume of the primary and secondary circuit IS NOT THE SAME. Max pressure of both circuits is identical at the MC.<P>A big chunk of the math you are trying to do is already in the article and/or the excel sheet. <A HREF="http://theogre.home.sprynet.com/brake_math95.xls" TARGET=_blank>http://theogre.home.sprynet.com/brake_math95.xls</A>  (This version will work with Excell 5/95 or newer.) It is very easy to alter the spread sheet. simply put the requisit diameters into the caliper and MC diameter boxes. All the other boxes will recalc themselves.<P>Acording to raybestos.....<P>Lateral Runout T.I.R. (Total Indicator Reading-1 Revolution); Front and Rear = .003 max for an 88 (.004 for all others.) [.030 would shake your teeth out.]<P>Max. Variance From Parallellism; Front and Rear = .0005 (all years.)<P>"Also ask why the GM MC would have a volume of 1.10429... in^3 <BR>when only .3365777... in^3 is required." 2 reasons... there is amgood margin for safety to allow a slight seal weep. I think you may have added wrong but I'm not quite sure where at the moment. Also the MC body is made to fit multiple GM vehicles which may need a little more volume.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||04-12-2001||07:54 PM||||Am I reading that right above????? are you going to replace the step bore MC with a straight bore?||||reg||1
Z||000007||fiero56||04-12-2001||09:22 PM||||Waiting for William to return my call. In the meantime, will go out and tear open my stock MC and see if it is straight or step...||||reg||1
Z||000008||fiero56||04-12-2001||10:56 PM||||Usually Willy is always right. But this time I think he messed up. I tore open my stock master, it is stepped(I wonder why MVMA doesn't mention that?), and I don't think stroke is the same for both circuits. William must be busy tonight. Will find out more tommorrow. <p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 04-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||04-12-2001||11:01 PM||||All stock Fiero MC are step. 84-87 36 & 25.4, 88 36 & 24.<P>My excel sheet doesn't include the 36mm bore because it is only used to increase the volume of the primary circuit. The actual brake work is done by the smaller bore. Since I was looking at main brake pressure the 36mm bore was irrelevant.<P>Also the 36mm bore isn't a simple cylinder calc for volume. You have to subtrack the volume occupied by the section between the 2 seals on the primay piston and the MC bore is getting smaller as the rear face is moved forward. It's a rather large injection of fluid from the 36mm bore but exactly how much is not easy to calc.<P>Myself, I'd never put a straight bore on an otherwise stock Fiero.<P>Unless you have a quationable source,,, I'd put a rebuilt OE MC on it. Rebuilt MC's are generally pretty reliable. They aren't that hard to rebuild when you are dealling in production levels. I tell individuals to avoid rebuilding it themselves because they can be easy to screw up if you haven't done them before.<P>If you are doing this to gain more rear brake to compensate a larger motor on an automatic tranny, then I'd look at reproportioning the OE system before trying diferant MC.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Indiana_resto_guy||04-12-2001||11:09 PM||||Fiero 56,<P>Theogre has already torn down a stock master and knows for sure what he speaks of.<BR>When I read the post earlier I thought that it looked a bit strange. Still can't put my finger on it, no time to do the physics of the thing at this time.<P>Theogre,<P>You read it as I did, looks like a straight bore which leads right on down the road with a bent brake pedal due to the additional<BR> 'push' to slow down. The area figures look out of whack for some reason to me.||||reg||1
Z||000011||fiero56||04-12-2001||11:39 PM||||Ogre, the MC is a rebuilt unit that never worked properly from the beginning. I had decided that I wasn't wasting my time with another, so was looking to go new. <P>I will give this one another try, making sure it is bench bled real well, before I try another, it only has a few thousand miles on it, but it really fu**** up my front pads and rotors since the rear system was doing $hit.||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||04-13-2001||05:25 PM||||Rear brake problem... Are you sure it's the MC and not the balance block and/or the prop valve in the balance block? A screwed up prop valve could easily cause weak rear brake.<P>Many rebuilts have a year replacement on them. Did you look into that? maybe you'd get a free one.<P>try vac or pressure bleeding. If you got air trapped in the line between the MC and balance it can be nearly imposible to get out by pumping the pedal.||||reg||1
Z||000013||fiero56||04-19-2001||12:20 PM||||Time to work on the Formula again...<P>I found the original plugs that came with the rebuilt master cylinder. I connected the master to its mount on the car, bench bled it, and plugged both ports. There is no leakdown or any air feel in the pedal. So I assume the cylinder is good, but I have bled and bled this system, and there is still always the feeling of air in the system where the pedal will slowly press to the end of its stroke. I can pump the pedal a few times and it will hold properly, but there must still be air in the system. I've been using the two-man pump the pedal bleeding. What am I missing? Is there any place I could rent a pressure or vac bleeder? I need this thing out of the garage by Saturday.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||theogre||04-19-2001||07:05 PM||||First.... you cannot "bench bleed" on the car. The MC must be level. Unless you are jacking the car way up in back that's not happening easily.<P>you can get a "cheap" vac bleed set thru most parts stores. At worst you have to buy a hand vac and the bleed bottle sperately. The cheap set runs about $20 last I saw one. A good hand vac with metal body is about $30 I think. the bleed jar is $10 or less...||||reg||1
Z||000015||fiero56||04-19-2001||08:02 PM||||The master was good and level. I used a bubble level against the bottom of the master and adjusted the rear hight of the car accordingly. I also just checked the angles of the brake components between my '86 and '88. The '86 "prop block"(I don't know the official term <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif">) sits at a much steeper angle than my '88 unit does. The '88 unit sits nearly level. Could this make getting air out of it more difficult since it sits level? I'll look into a bleeder. I've never heard of them working properly/well. Will look for a steel body unit.||||reg||1
